ain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 57\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>57\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 64\u00b0F, and the record is 86\u00b0F (1955). The low of <strong>37\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Little Rock has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Little Rock's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Little Rock has set <strong class=\"sig\">33 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 67 that chance alone would give, and <strong>99 record highs</strong>, more than the 67 that chance alone would give. The 1950s hold the most record highs of any decade (49). Little Rock's all-time high is 114\u00b0F, on August 3, 2011.","t_chg":"Little Rock's nights have warmed since 1986. Its days warmed over the long run.","p_chg":"Across 146 complete years: Little Rock's average low shows no clear change: \u22120.05\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.11 to 0.00) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero. Little Rock's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.21\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.16 to +0.27), about +3.1\u00b0F since 1880. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.54\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.21 to +0.88) and highs show no clear change. The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 2 of 12 months; January and April fell. Highs rose in 10 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Little Rock's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.20 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 50.4 inches a year. The wettest year was 2009 (81.79 in); the driest, 1963 (28.26 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 13 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}